Shoals.Stochastic: the time horizon must be finite; a non-finite horizon makes the log drift and sigma * sqrt(t) non-finite, so every path value would be NaN
Assumption: a non-finite time horizon names no path either. The two branches of
checked_horizon carry DIFFERENT diagnostics on purpose, for the reason
count_params_finite records one line above them: `gte(nan, 0.0)` is false, so a
single ordering check would report a NaN horizon as a negative one and name the
wrong cause.

`+inf` is refused on a measured ground rather than for tidiness. The log drift
and `sigma * sqrt(t)` are then both `+inf`, so `drift + vol_sqrt_t * z` is
`inf - inf` for every negative draw: measured on shoals@bba1a22 at n = 8,
seed 7, gbm_terminal returned NaN for every path at t = +inf, exactly as it did
at t = -1.

Scope of this pair: all nine entry points route through the one
checked_horizon, so these two files pin the non-finite DIAGNOSTICS, not
per-sampler coverage of them. The per-sampler files beside them pin that each
sampler actually calls the guard, which is the property a single shared fixture
could not establish.
